<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>
  <!--
    ============================================================================
    Why this file exists
    ============================================================================
    Without it, restore resolves against whatever the machine's *user-level*
    NuGet.config happens to say. That makes the build a function of the developer's
    machine: a corporate mirror, a private feed, or a leftover source added for some
    other project can all change what this repository restores, with no diff here to
    show for it. A build that is supposed to be reproducible should not have its
    package sources decided by a file that is not in it.

    <clear /> drops every source inherited from a parent config (the machine-level
    and user-level files) and leaves only what is listed below. That is the point:
    after this, "where did this package come from" has exactly one answer.

    Why nuget.org is the only source
    ================================
    All 42 direct PackageReferences and every transitive dependency resolve from
    nuget.org today, including the ones that are not a NuGet package at all:
    FreeSO.HandEvaluator 1.2.0, Portable.JWT 1.0.5, AWSSDK.* 3.3.x, Octokit 0.36.0,
    Mina 2.0.12, System.Data.SQLite.Core 1.0.119. Verified against
    Simitone.Windows/obj/project.assets.json, whose restore.sources lists nuget.org
    and nothing else. So <clear /> changes nothing about what is restored today and
    removes the possibility of it being different tomorrow.

    What is deliberately NOT here
    =============================
    No <packageSourceMapping>. The audit's suggestion to pin the unmaintained
    packages (Mina, xxHashSharp, Portable.JWT, Portable.BouncyCastle, Open.Nat,
    FreeSO.HandEvaluator) to nuget.org explicitly is the right end state, but
    mapping only bites once every package is mapped, and getting that list wrong
    turns into restore errors. With one source it is currently documentation.
    No <globalPackagesFolder>: that belongs to the machine, not to the repository,
    and overriding it here is the same category of mistake as the
    NuGetGlobalPackages override that used to live in Simitone.Windows.csproj.
    No credentials or apiKeys: nothing in this repository needs an authenticated feed.
  -->
  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
  </packageSources>
</configuration>
